1. Platform Scope and Navigation

•	Primary navigation order: Dashboard → Pipelines → Deployments → Infrastructure → Kubernetes → Applications → Security → AI Operations → Automation → Observability → Cost Management → Reports → Settings.
•	Global shell: product wordmark, workspace selector, universal search, environment indicator, notifications, help, user menu, breadcrumb, page title, contextual actions, and persistent left navigation.
•	Shared states: loading, empty, onboarding, syncing, populated, degraded, permission denied, partial failure, offline, and destructive-action confirmation.
•	Design baseline: responsive desktop-first layout; accessible color contrast; keyboard navigation; visible focus; tooltips for icons; timestamps in user locale; skeleton loaders instead of layout shifts.
•	Tenancy: every API request and event must carry organization, workspace, project, environment, actor, correlation ID, and authorization context.

2. Run Pipeline and Project Onboarding

•	RunPipeline_EmptyState: detect zero projects; show Create Project and Import Project. Do not show pipeline controls until a project exists.
•	ProjectSetup_Modal: create a native project or import from GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, Gerrit, Harness, Azure Repos, or custom Git URL.
•	Authentication: use provider OAuth/App installation where available; display requested scopes before redirect; store tokens in the secrets service, never in browser storage.
•	Repository selection: searchable, paginated list with repository type, default branch, access state, selected count, and bulk selection.
•	Import orchestration: clone → language detection → Dockerfile/IaC detection → SBOM generation → security scan → pipeline-template preparation. Stream progress via server-sent events or WebSocket; persist resumable job state.
•	Pipeline setup: Standard auto-detected template, Advanced editable steps, or Custom YAML. Validate schema, permissions, referenced secrets, and runner compatibility before creation.
•	Ready state: confirm repo connection, active webhooks, CI/CD tokens, and security scanners; actions are Run Pipeline Now and View Pipeline Configuration.
•	Core endpoints: POST /projects, POST /connections/source, GET /repositories, POST /imports, GET /jobs/{id}, POST /pipelines, POST /pipelines/{id}/runs.
•	Events: project.created, repository.import.started|completed|failed, pipeline.created, pipeline.run.started|passed|failed|blocked.

3. Pipelines, Deployments, and Artifacts

•	Pipelines: CI, CD, security gates, SBOM, vulnerability scans, run history, stages, logs, artifacts, approvals, retries, cancellation, and YAML configuration.
•	Deploy_Start: deploy latest pipeline artifact, a specific version, or an item from a container/Helm/artifact registry. Show application, environment, and prior deployment status.
•	Environment selection: Production, Staging, QA, Development, or custom Kubernetes/VM targets with cluster, region, nodes, last deployment, and compliance state.
•	Strategy: Rolling, Blue/Green, Canary, Recreate, or Shadow. Present risk, estimated rollout time, and AI recommendation; require explicit confirmation for high-risk production changes.
•	Artifact selection: search and filter by version, digest, type, provenance, signature, security result, SBOM availability, and creation time. Deploy immutable digest, not mutable tag.
•	Security gates: SBOM, CVE, dependencies, container, IaC, secrets, compliance, and AI threat score. Failed gates block by default. Override requires RBAC, justification, approver, expiry, and audit entry.
•	Configuration: replicas, requests/limits, variables, Vault secrets, ConfigMaps, ingress, HPA, rollback threshold, YAML, Helm values, and manifest editor. Redact secrets in UI and logs.
•	Execution: environment preparation → artifact pull → pod creation → health checks → traffic routing → post-deployment verification. Stream logs, events, pod state, allocation, traffic weight, and anomaly signals.
•	Verification and summary: health, errors, latency, logs, metrics, traces, security re-scan, AI anomaly result, version, strategy, duration, gate results, audit record, and rollback action.
•	Core endpoints/events: GET /artifacts; POST /deployments; GET /deployments/{id}/stream; POST /deployments/{id}/rollback; deployment.started|stage.changed|verified|failed|rolled_back.

4. Infrastructure, Kubernetes, and Applications

•	Infrastructure empty state: detect no connected resources and offer Open Pipeline Control. Providers: Kubernetes, AWS, Azure, GCP, VM, bare metal/on-prem, and custom SSH/API endpoint.
•	Connection: collect cluster name, API server, authentication method, namespaces, and previewed RBAC. Validate credentials server-side and return least-privilege remediation.
•	Agent installation: Kubernetes Helm chart, VM binary, or cloud IAM/Lambda agent. Use short-lived enrollment tokens, signed packages, rotation, heartbeat, and version compatibility checks.
•	Synchronization: connect → validate → install agent → nodes → workloads → capacity → security posture. Jobs must be idempotent and resumable.
•	Populated workspace: clusters, nodes, workloads, CPU/memory/storage capacity, networking, security posture, connected services, sync freshness, and Pipeline Control for deployments, rollouts, rollbacks, checks, traffic, and autoscaling.
•	Kubernetes module: clusters, nodes, pods, workloads, HPA, ingress/load balancers, namespaces, events, manifests, and policy status. Destructive actions require scoped permissions and confirmation.
•	Applications module: microservices, APIs, versions, dependencies, runtime security, ownership, environments, SLOs, deployed artifact, health, and related pipelines.
•	Core endpoints/events: POST /infrastructure/connections; POST /agents/enroll; GET /infrastructure/sync/{id}; GET /clusters; GET /workloads; agent.connected|heartbeat.missed; cluster.synced; workload.health.changed.

5. Security Center and AI Operations

•	Security onboarding: connect SAST, DAST, container scanning, SBOM/dependencies, IaC security, cloud posture, OPA/Kyverno policy engines, and runtime threat detection.
•	SAST: provider, authentication, repositories, branches, detected languages, OWASP/CWE/custom rules. Support baseline suppression with owner, reason, and expiry.
•	Container security: registry URL/provider, authentication, namespaces, image discovery, SBOM generation, signature/provenance verification, and continuous re-scan.
•	IaC security: Terraform/Helm/Kubernetes, repositories, CIS/NIST/custom policies, drift detection, pull-request annotations, and deploy-time enforcement.
•	Security sync: connect sources, scan assets, generate SBOMs, sync posture/policies, build attack graph, calculate AI threat score. Expose live progress and per-source errors.
•	Populated Security Center: critical/high alerts, compliance score, policy violations, vulnerabilities, AI risk score, alert timeline, MITRE ATT&CK mapping, CVE exploitability, affected components, fixes, SBOM viewer, policy findings, runtime alerts, and attack paths.
•	Attack paths: visualize entry point → lateral movement → impact; every edge must cite observed evidence or modeled assumption. Show confidence, blast radius, and remediation priority.
•	AI Operations: risk scoring, anomaly detection, predictive alerts, and generated reports. AI output is advisory, must show confidence and evidence, and cannot bypass policy or RBAC.
•	Endpoints/events: POST /security/connections; GET /findings; GET /sboms; GET /attack-paths; POST /findings/{id}/suppress; finding.created|updated|resolved; policy.blocked; risk.score.changed.

6. Automation Workspace

•	Sources: pipeline events, deployment events, infrastructure signals, Kubernetes events, application releases, observability alerts, security findings, cost alerts, and custom webhooks.
•	Pipeline automation: select pipelines; trigger on success, failure, or gate block; actions include notify, rollback, run job, and open ticket.
•	Deployment automation: strategy, health, rollback, and traffic-routing triggers. Guard production actions with approvals and concurrency controls.
•	Kubernetes automation: pod crashes, node pressure, autoscaling, and admission events with namespace/cluster scope.
•	Observability automation: log patterns, metric thresholds, trace anomalies, and imported alert rules.
•	Security automation: CVE severity, policy violations, runtime threats, auto-block deployment, and auto-patch. High-impact actions require RBAC and optional human approval.
•	Workflow model: versioned graph of trigger → conditions → branches → actions. Support draft, validation, simulation, publish, pause, rollback, cloning, and history.
•	Execution guarantees: idempotency key, retry policy, exponential backoff, timeout, dead-letter queue, deduplication, rate limits, and maximum recursion depth.
•	Populated workspace: active workflows, remediation actions, delivery controls, trigger sources, success rate, AI score, drag-and-drop builder, workflow library, remediation feed, event stream, and AI recommendations.
•	Endpoints/events: POST /automation/connections; POST /workflows; POST /workflows/{id}/simulate|publish; GET /executions; automation.triggered|action.started|completed|failed.

7. Observability and Cost Management

•	Observability: logs, metrics, traces, dashboards, alerts, service map, SLOs, error budgets, retention, and telemetry-source health.
•	Correlation: propagate trace ID, deployment ID, pipeline run ID, service, version, environment, and tenant across events and telemetry.
•	Queries: time range, environment, service, severity, version, region, cluster, and saved views. Long-running searches execute asynchronously and are cancellable.
•	Alerting: threshold, anomaly, absence-of-data, and composite rules with routing, silences, maintenance windows, deduplication, and escalation.
•	Cost Management: cloud spend, Kubernetes allocation, resource optimization, forecasts, budgets, alerts, tags, ownership, idle resources, and unit economics.
•	Cost safety: recommendations must show expected savings, confidence, performance risk, affected resources, and approval path. Never resize or terminate automatically without policy authorization.
•	Endpoints/events: GET /telemetry/logs|metrics|traces; POST /alerts/rules; GET /cost/spend|forecast|recommendations; alert.fired|resolved; budget.threshold.exceeded; optimization.created.

8. Reports, Settings, and Administration

•	Reports: security, compliance, deployment, cost, pipeline/DORA, reliability, and custom reports. Support filters, scheduled generation, access-controlled sharing, export, retention, and signed report metadata.
•	User Management: invite, deactivate, suspend, SCIM status, SSO identity, MFA state, groups, last activity, and service accounts. Never expose secrets or raw authentication data.
•	Roles & Permissions: organization, workspace, project, environment, and resource scopes. Provide built-in roles plus custom policies; show effective permissions and deny reasons.
•	API Keys: named keys with scopes, owner, creation, last use, expiry, allowed IPs, rotation, and revocation. Display secret once; store only secure hash.
•	Integrations: Git providers, registries, cloud, Kubernetes, Vault, ticketing, chat, SIEM, observability, and webhooks. Include health, last sync, scopes, retry, and disconnect.
•	Audit Logs: immutable actor/action/resource/result records with timestamp, tenant, source IP, correlation ID, before/after metadata, export, retention, and integrity verification.
•	Settings: organization profile, branding, environments, policies, notifications, retention, feature flags, agent channels, regional residency, billing/cost controls, and danger zone.
•	Administrative safeguards: step-up authentication for sensitive changes, dual approval for policy bypass, confirmation text for destructive actions, and complete audit coverage.

9. Engineering Architecture and Data Contracts

•	Frontend: component-based web application with typed routes, shared design system, query cache, normalized error handling, feature flags, localization, and generated API client.
•	Backend: API gateway plus domain services for identity, projects, source connections, pipelines, artifacts, deployments, infrastructure, security, automation, telemetry, cost, reports, and audit.
•	Async platform: event bus, workflow/job engine, scheduler, stream gateway, notification service, and dead-letter processing. Every command returns a stable operation ID.
•	Canonical resource envelope: id, tenantId, workspaceId, projectId, name, type, status, labels, createdAt, updatedAt, createdBy, version, links.
•	Operation envelope: operationId, resourceId, state, percent, currentStep, startedAt, updatedAt, completedAt, error, warnings, retryable, correlationId.
•	Error contract: code, message, userMessage, fieldErrors, correlationId, retryable, remediationUrl. UI must not render raw stack traces.
•	Pagination: cursor-based lists with stable sorting, filters, total estimate where available, and cancellation.
•	Versioning: additive API evolution by default; explicit version headers; schema registry for events; compatibility tests for agents and CLIs.
•	Secrets: references only in application data; encryption in transit and at rest; redaction in logs; expiry and rotation; no secret values in analytics.

10. Security, Quality, and Release Requirements

•	Authentication: OIDC/SAML SSO, MFA, short-lived sessions, secure cookies, CSRF protection, device/session management, and service-account separation.
•	Authorization: server-side policy enforcement on every request; deny by default; resource-scoped RBAC/ABAC; no security decisions based only on hidden UI controls.
•	Supply chain: signed builds, provenance, dependency pinning, SBOM, secret scanning, branch protection, artifact signatures, and release attestations.
•	Accessibility: WCAG-aligned contrast, semantic labels, keyboard operation, focus order, screen-reader announcements for progress/errors, and no color-only status.
•	Performance targets: responsive shell, paginated data, virtualized large lists, progressive charts, backpressure for streams, and cached metadata with explicit freshness.
•	Testing pyramid: unit tests for logic/components; contract tests for APIs/events; integration tests for providers; end-to-end tests for onboarding, pipeline, deployment, security, automation, and settings; load and chaos tests for streams/jobs.
•	Required E2E journeys: import repo and run first pipeline; deploy approved artifact; block failed security gate; connect cluster and sync; create automation rule; investigate alert; generate report; rotate API key; inspect audit entry.
•	Release gates: tests pass, no unresolved critical vulnerabilities, migrations validated, rollback tested, observability dashboards ready, runbooks updated, feature flag plan approved.

11. Definition of Done

•	All specified screens, empty states, onboarding paths, populated states, errors, permissions, and destructive confirmations are implemented.
•	API schemas, events, authorization rules, audit coverage, and telemetry are documented and contract-tested.
•	Loading, timeout, retry, partial failure, stale data, offline, and reconnect behavior are verified.
•	Secrets are never exposed; sensitive actions require correct approval; security-gate overrides are attributable and time-bound.
•	Accessibility, responsive behavior, localization, performance budgets, browser support, and end-to-end journeys pass.
•	Operational dashboards, alerts, SLOs, support runbooks, rollback procedure, migration plan, and ownership are ready before production release.
•	Final navigation acceptance: a user can move coherently from Run Pipeline through Deployments, Infrastructure, Kubernetes, Applications, Security, AI Operations, Automation, Observability, Cost Management, Reports, and Settings without losing project, environment, or authorization context.


